  {"id":"difficult-airway-algo-1","topic":"Difficult airway algorithms","domain":"Airway management (assessment & prediction, supraglottic & ETT devices, laryngoscopy & fiberoptic techniques, surgical airway)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"What is the core principle that all difficult airway society algorithms share?","answer":"Discontinue repeated failed technique — algorithms discourage performing the same failed maneuver repeatedly; escalate to a different approach or rescue strategy","rationale":"Repeated attempts at failed intubation cause escalating trauma, edema, and bleeding that convert a manageable difficult airway into a cannot-intubate-cannot-oxygenate emergency.","bloom":"analyze","source":[{"book":"Morgan & Mikhail","page":531}],"confusable_with":"unlimited attempts before declaring failure"},
