  {"id":"difficult-airway-algo-3","topic":"Difficult airway algorithms","domain":"Airway management (assessment & prediction, supraglottic & ETT devices, laryngoscopy & fiberoptic techniques, surgical airway)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"What technique is recommended when post-induction laryngoscopy proves difficult?","answer":"Combined video laryngoscope plus intubating bougie — the two tools together often succeed when either alone fails","rationale":"The video laryngoscope improves glottic visualization while the bougie navigates the angled path; together they overcome the limitations of each device used independently.","bloom":"apply","source":[{"book":"Morgan & Mikhail","page":535}],"confusable_with":"repeated direct laryngoscopy attempts"},
  {"id":"difficult-airway-algo-4","topic":"Difficult airway algorithms","domain":"Airway management (assessment & prediction, supraglottic & ETT devices, laryngoscopy & fiberoptic techniques, surgical airway)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"In the pediatric difficult airway, why is nasal cannula oxygen particularly useful during fiberoptic intubation?","answer":"Nasal cannula oxygen in infants and children increases the time available for fiberoptic intubation by providing apneic oxygenation","rationale":"Apneic oxygenation via nasal cannula provides passive oxygen delivery to the lungs during apnea, extending the safe apnea time for instrument manipulation.","bloom":"apply","source":[{"book":"Miller/Baby Miller","page":308}],"confusable_with":"nasal cannula as adequate ventilation device"},
  {"id":"difficult-airway-algo-5","topic":"Difficult airway algorithms","domain":"Airway management (assessment & prediction, supraglottic & ETT devices, laryngoscopy & fiberoptic techniques, surgical airway)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"What is the maximum recommended number of attempts for each technique in a difficult airway scenario?","answer":"No more than 3 attempts for each technique (facemask, LMA, ETT) before escalating to a different approach","rationale":"Limiting attempts prevents progressive airway trauma and maintains the option to escalate safely before oxygenation becomes critical.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"Stanford CA-1","page":44}],"confusable_with":"no limit on intubation attempts before declaring failure"}
