  {"id":"difficult-airway-obs-2","topic":"Difficult and failed airway in obstetrics","domain":"Obstetric anesthesia (maternal physiology, labor analgesia, cesarean anesthesia, obstetric emergencies)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"What premedication regimen reduces aspiration risk in high-risk obstetric patients undergoing elective cesarean?","answer":"Oral omeprazole 40 mg at night and in the morning; glycopyrrolate 0.1 mg IV reduces secretions (anticholinergics theoretically reduce lower esophageal sphincter tone but this is managed)","rationale":"Pregnant patients have increased aspiration risk from reduced lower esophageal sphincter tone and increased gastric volume; acid suppression reduces severity if aspiration occurs.","bloom":"apply","source":[{"book":"Morgan & Mikhail","page":1407}],"confusable_with":"sodium citrate alone as adequate prophylaxis"},
  {"id":"difficult-airway-obs-3","topic":"Difficult and failed airway in obstetrics","domain":"Obstetric anesthesia (maternal physiology, labor analgesia, cesarean anesthesia, obstetric emergencies)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"When performing rapid-sequence induction with cricoid pressure, what specific laryngoscopic assessment guides intubation?","answer":"The Cormack-Lehane classification of the laryngoscopic view — Grade III or IV views are associated with difficult intubation","rationale":"Grading the initial laryngoscopic view guides the decision to use video laryngoscopy, bougie assistance, or alternative airway devices before oxygen desaturation occurs.","bloom":"apply","source":[{"book":"Miller/Baby Miller","page":291}],"confusable_with":"Mallampati score during active RSI"},
